ZTEXT001000009f8973d122d0000000
Zero prepared updater format 1

Store-owned names:
  zero.updates                 append-only 256-byte records, max 4096 records
  version-XXXXXXXX.iso         8 lowercase hexadecimal digits, u32 counter
  .payload.part                exclusive current-operation temporary image
  .update-lock                 exclusive cooperating-writer lock
No release metadata field is interpreted as a pathname.

Inbox-owned names:
  system.iso                   exact independently SHA256-verified image
  release.meta                 one record, seq1, previous0, highest=current
No signature or authenticated origin is implied by these files.

Record offsets (all integers u64 little-endian; digests raw SHA256 bytes):
  0     magic 0x313030445055525a (ZRUPD001)
  8     format 1
  16    sequence 1..4096, increments exactly one
  24    selected PREPARED release counter 1..0xffffffff
  32    previous selected counter, or0 on first record
  40    highest counter ever accepted; never decreases
  48    selected ISO byte length
  56    selected ISO SHA256, 32 bytes
  88    previous ISO byte length, or0
  96    previous ISO SHA256, 32 bytes; all zero when no previous
  128   state 1 (PREPARED)
  136   boot activation flags 0 (not activated)
  144   reserved 96 bytes, all zero
  240   FNV-1a64 of bytes0..239 (offset basis14695981039346656037,
        multiply1099511628211 modulo2^64 after XOR of each byte)
  248   commit marker 0x3154494d4d4f435a (ZCOMMIT1)

A later entry's previous tuple must equal the preceding entry's selected
tuple. A new higher selection sets highest to that new counter. A rollback
must select the preceding previous tuple and retain highest. Format, chain,
reserved bytes, marker and corruption checksum must all validate. Partial
records are rejected by status/verify/mutations. Explicit inspect/recover may
validate a nonempty complete prefix and report the 1..255 remaining tail bytes;
recover writes that prefix only into a new exclusive directory. It never
truncates the source, ignores a whole invalid record, or drops the high-water
history. Current/previous images are copied; older images remain in source. Current and previous ISO digests
are fully recomputed before status or a new mutation can succeed. Historical
unselected payloads are retained but not all rehashed on every operation.

Payload and journal file writes are flushed before success/publication. Parent
directory synchronization and power-loss ordering are not guaranteed. Snapshot
recovery introduces no new record format and does not change format 1.
